# NON-REGRESSION PIN — ⚠ GREEN BEFORE AND AFTER THE ALIAS-RESOLUTION FIX.
# This fixture is NOT red-verifiable against the pre-fix compiler, and it is
# labelled as such deliberately. What it pins is stated below; do not read it
# as coverage for the alias-spelling defect (the four
# `cow_alias_spelled_*` fixtures carry that).
#
# CELL: TEMPORAL ORDER — the alias is bound BEFORE the root is reassigned,
# and the view is taken AFTER.
#
#     Vector[String] alias = v      # alias aliases v's storage
#     v = []                        # reassign: severs, alias takes its own copy
#     String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()   # view taken AFTER the sever
#     ... grow the NEW v ...
#     print(s)                      # must still be the OLD element
#
# WHAT IT PINS: resolving a collection identity to its storage owner is only
# sound because the sever runs `unset_ownership` on the alias BEFORE
# materializing it. Once `alias` is no longer tagged `Alias(v)`, root
# resolution terminates at `alias` itself, so the view taken after the sever
# is correctly attributed to `alias`'s own buffer and NOT to the new `v`.
# Remove that `unset_ownership` and this program mis-attributes the view.
# That invariant was load-bearing but unpinned before this fixture; the fix
# now depends on it.
#
# BREAK-THE-MECHANISM CHECK: deleting the `unset_ownership(builder, alias)`
# call that precedes the materialize in `cow_sever_all_aliases_from` is the
# way to see this go red.
#
# The two lengths are printed as well as the string: they pin that the sever
# gave `alias` its own 1-element copy while `v` went on to 65, i.e. that CoW
# value semantics held across the reassignment.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Vector[String] alias = v
    v = []
    v.push(mk("new", "one"))
    String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push(mk("fill", "er"))
        i = i + 1
    print(s)
    print(alias.len())
    print(v.len())
